<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agaw people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Agaw are a Cushitic-speaking people of the northern and central highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, among the oldest inhabitants of the region. An Agaw dynasty, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagwe-dynasty">Zagwe</a>, ruled Ethiopia from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lasta">Lasta</a> from about the 12th century until 1270 and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">rock-hewn churches</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela">Lalibela</a>. Agaw languages survive in several communities, though many Agaw have shifted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigrinya-language">Tigrinya</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agaw-people.jpg" alt="Agaw people" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abrham Zelalem · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Injibara-az-0002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
